---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Goplus 2 in 1 Treadmill Review (2026): SuperFit Specs and Limits";
const description =
  "The Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1 is a $150-$200 folding pad with a rail that unlocks a 7.5 mph mode. What is confirmed, the conflicting warranty, the generic listing title and who it suits.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "goplus-superfit-2-in-1",
    name: "Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1",
    category: "2-in-1 Folding Treadmill and Walking Pad",
    badge: "Budget 2-in-1",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Goplus+2+in+1+Folding+Treadmill+2.25HP+Superfit&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$150-$200",
    priceBounds: {"low":150,"high":200},
    specs: "About 40 x 16 in. belt · 0.6-2.5 mph rail down, up to 7.5 mph rail up · 265 lb capacity · about 5 in. folded",
    pros: [
      "Inexpensive: $149.99 on Costway’s store and $189.99-$199.99 from the brand’s Amazon storefront on the day we checked",
      "Folds to about 5 inches thick, so it slides under a bed or sofa",
      "The fold-up rail doubles as a speed switch: a gentle walking mode under a desk, a running mode up to 7.5 mph when locked upright",
      "Built-in Bluetooth speaker and phone holder, and sold nearly fully assembled",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Walking mode is capped at about 2.5 mph, slow for brisk walkers who want to stay under a desk",
      "Controlled only by a small remote; three of the four hands-on reviews we read warned that losing it leaves the machine unusable",
      "The side handles bolt on and must be removed to fold the pad flat",
      "Warranty depends on where you buy: 365 days on Costway’s store, 3 months in the Amazon listing data",
      "A belt of about 40 inches is short for running and for taller walkers",
    ],
    bottomLine: "A sensible budget buy for slow desk walking with the option of a short jog, bought from the seller with the longest written warranty. Not a running treadmill, and not for anyone who wants buttons on the machine.",
    description: "The Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1 is a low folding treadmill with a handrail that folds down for under-desk walking and locks upright for a faster mode. Goplus’s Amazon listings and Costway’s own listing agree, and independent hands-on reviews confirm, that the belt is about 40 by 16 inches (Costway’s specification table gives 39.3 by 16), that the pad is capped at about 2.5 mph with the rail down and runs up to 7.5 mph with it up, that it is rated for 265 lb, and that it folds to about 5 inches thick. It has a 2.25 HP motor with no continuous rating given, an LED display on the deck, a Bluetooth speaker, a phone holder and a small handheld remote, which is the only way to start it and change speed. Newer versions connect to the Gymax app. Its weight is given as 69.5 lb, 73 lb and 74 lb in different places. The warranty is where sources diverge most: 365 days on Costway’s store, 3 months in Amazon’s listing data.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Reviews", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/reviews/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Goplus 2-in-1 Treadmill", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/goplus-2-in-1-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Goplus 2-in-1 the same as the SuperFit treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, in most listings. Goplus sells the 2-in-1 under the SuperFit name, and Costway, the company behind Goplus, sells the same machine on its own store. Several versions exist, including an original single-display model, a newer Gymax-app model, a dual-display model with a 9 mph mode and a larger 4.75 HP machine, so check the model number.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run on the Goplus 2-in-1 treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Briefly and lightly. With the rail locked upright it runs up to 7.5 mph, but the belt is only about 40 by 16 inches, and independent reviewers found taller users had to shorten their stride. Treat it as a walking pad with a jogging option rather than a running treadmill, and clip the safety key to your clothing.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How fast does the Goplus 2-in-1 go in walking mode?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "About 2.5 mph with the rail folded down, which is the under-desk mode. That is a comfortable typing pace for many people but below a brisk walk. To go faster you have to raise and lock the rail, which takes the pad out from under a desk. Some reviews quote the same limits in km/h, as 1 to 4.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the 2-in-1 folding treadmill with RC Bluetooth speaker and LED display a Goplus?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually. That title is Costway’s catalogue name for the Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1 family, copied by resellers, often with a Costway code beginning SP37. At least one shop uses a near-identical title for a different 2.0 HP machine rated for 250 lb, so check the model number, and buy from Costway or the Goplus storefront for brand support.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What warranty does the Goplus 2-in-1 have?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on where you buy it. Costway’s store advertised a 365-day warranty on the day we checked, while Amazon’s listing data for Goplus 2-in-1 models gives 3 months, and one independent review reported a three-month term on the Goplus website. Save the warranty wording on the day you buy and ask the seller if it is unclear.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use the Goplus 2-in-1 without the remote?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not in normal use. There are no speed or start buttons on the machine; the small remote starts it and changes speed, and the safety key only stops it. Every hands-on review flagged this. Keep the remote in a fixed place, keep a spare battery, and ask the seller about a replacement remote before you need one.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-14",
      dateModified: "2026-10-14",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 1,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/goplus-2-in-1-treadmill/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-14",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-14",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "goplus 2 in 1 treadmill",
      "2-in-1 folding treadmill with rc bluetooth speaker and led display",
      "goplus superfit treadmill",
      "goplus 2 in 1 folding treadmill",
      "costway 2 in 1 treadmill",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Who It Suits", link: "#who-it-suits" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-goplus2in1treadmill" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-goplus2in1treadmill)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Model Review
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Goplus 2-in-1 Treadmill <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Review: The SuperFit</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          The Goplus 2-in-1, sold as the SuperFit, is one of the longest-running budget folding treadmills: a 5-inch-thin pad with a handrail that folds up to unlock a running mode. It is a fair buy for slow desk walking and short jogs, provided you understand its 2.5 mph walking cap, its remote-only controls and a warranty that depends on where you buy it.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#goplus-superfit-2-in-1" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            What Is Confirmed
          </a>
          <a href="#who-it-suits" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Who It Suits
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 14, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="2.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          14 October 2026 we checked the Goplus 2-in-1 listings on Amazon (brand, model numbers,
          weights, warranty field and current seller), the same machine’s listing on Costway’s own
          store, and four independent reviews of the SuperFit 2-in-1 that report hands-on use, then
          compared them with the figures already on our walking pad and low-profile roundups. The
          card below gives only figures on which the brand’s listings and at least one independent
          review agree. Where sources conflict — the machine’s weight, the top speed, and above all
          the warranty — this page says so rather than choosing. We also searched the CPSC recall
          database for Goplus and Costway. We have not tested the Goplus 2-in-1 ourselves. Prices
          are those shown on the day we checked and change often. We earn a commission if you buy
          through the Amazon link on this page; it does not change what we say about the machine.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Is the Goplus 2-in-1 Treadmill Any Good? The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#which-machine" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Which Machine Is the “2-in-1 Folding Treadmill With RC Bluetooth Speaker and LED Display”?</a>
          <a href="#specifications" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Goplus 2-in-1 Specifications: Confirmed, Conflicting and Claimed</a>
          <a href="#rail-and-modes" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The Fold-Up Rail: How the Two Modes Work</a>
          <a href="#controls-and-display" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Controls, Display, Speaker and App</a>
          <a href="#owner-reports" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. What Independent Reviewers and Owners Consistently Report</a>
          <a href="#warranty-and-buying" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Warranty, Returns and Where to Buy</a>
          <a href="#keeping-it-running" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Keeping a Goplus 2-in-1 Running</a>
          <a href="#who-it-suits" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Who the Goplus 2-in-1 Suits, and the Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The Goplus 2-in-1 folding treadmill has been sold under several names and model numbers for
          years, and its listing title — some version of “2-in-1 folding treadmill with remote
          control, Bluetooth speaker and LED display” — has been copied so widely that it now reads
          like a product category. This review pins down which machine that title usually means,
          sets out the figures that the brand and independent reviewers agree on, flags the ones
          they do not, and says plainly who should buy it and who should spend more.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is the Goplus 2-in-1 Treadmill Any Good? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The Goplus 2-in-1 treadmill is a reasonable budget choice for slow under-desk
            walking with an occasional short jog, and a poor one for brisk desk walking, regular
            running or anyone who wants long warranty cover.</strong> Its core figures can be
            confirmed: a belt of about 40 by 16 inches, a 2.5 mph cap with the rail folded and up to
            7.5 mph with it raised, a 265 lb rating and a folded thickness of about 5 inches.
          </p>
          <p>
            Independent reviewers who have used it agree on its strengths and weaknesses more than
            they disagree. It is compact, simple and quick to set up. It is also controlled only by a
            small remote, its display sits low on the deck, its handles have
            to be unbolted before it folds flat, and its short belt makes taller users shorten their
            stride. The figure that varies most is the warranty, which runs from three months to a
            year depending on the listing.
          </p>
          <p>
            So buy it on those terms. At $150 to $200 it is fair value for a slim pad that can also
            take a jog. Buy it from the seller whose written warranty is longest, keep the remote
            somewhere safe, and if you mainly want to walk briskly at a desk, choose a pad whose
            walking mode reaches 3.5 to 4 mph instead.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Product cards -->
      <div class="not-prose">
        {
          PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-3">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-white font-black text-xl leading-tight m-0">
                      {index + 1}. {product.name}
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{product.category}</p>
                  </div>
                  <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
                    {product.badge}
                  </span>
                </div>
                <div class="p-6">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4 flex-wrap">
                    <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400">Typical street price</span>
                    <span class="ml-auto font-black text-gray-900">{product.priceRange}</span>
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400 mb-4">{product.specs}</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6" set:html={product.description} />
                  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-100 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <h4 class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-700 mt-0 mb-2">What We Like</h4>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.pros.map((pro) => (
                          <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{pro}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <h4 class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mt-0 mb-2">What We Don't Like</h4>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.cons.map((con) => (
                          <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{con}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="border-t border-gray-100 pt-4">
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold mb-4">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
                      <a href={product.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
                        {product.amazonLabel} &rarr;
                      </a>
                      {product.reviewUrl && (
                        <a href={product.reviewUrl} class="text-[#0F62FE] font-bold no-underline text-sm hover:underline">
                          Read our full {product.name} review &rarr;
                        </a>
                      )}
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="which-machine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Which Machine Is the “2-in-1 Folding Treadmill With RC Bluetooth Speaker and LED Display”?</h2>
<p>
            The long title in the heading is one of the most searched names in <a href="/best-budget-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>budget treadmills</a>, and
            it is worth knowing where it comes from. It is the catalogue name Costway uses for this
            machine family. Costway is the company behind Goplus and other house brands such as
            Gymax, and Goplus uses the SuperFit name for this line; one independent reviewer describes Costway as Goplus’s parent and
            lists Gymax, Slickblue and Tangkula alongside it. Costway sells the same 2-in-1 under its
            own name on its store, under Goplus on Amazon, and through drop-shipping partners who copy
            its listing title word for word.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is why you will find “2-in-1 Folding Treadmill with RC Bluetooth Speaker LED Display”
            on small web shops, marketplace listings and resale posts, often with a Costway product
            code beginning SP37 in the listing. In most cases it is the Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1 or a
            close sibling, sold by a reseller. In a few cases it is not: at least one shop using a
            near-identical title describes a 2.0 HP machine rated for 250 lb, which is a different
            product. And a reseller is not Costway, so its warranty and returns are whatever that
            reseller offers.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The versions you will see</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The original SuperFit 2-in-1 (SP37590).</strong> Single LED display, 2.25 HP,
              about 40 by 16-inch belt, walking mode to about 2.5 mph and running mode to about
              7.5 mph. This is the version most independent reviews describe.</li>
            <li><strong>The current Gymax-app version (SP37914, SP37913).</strong> The same deck,
              speeds, 265 lb rating and 5-inch fold, now pairing with the Gymax app. This is the one
              sold on Costway’s store and the main Goplus Amazon listing on the day we checked.</li>
            <li><strong>The dual-display version.</strong> A Goplus listing with two LED displays
              gives a 0.6-9 mph running mode and the same 2.5 mph walking cap. Its figures differ
              from the others, so check which version a listing is for.</li>
            <li><strong>The 4.75 HP 2-in-1 (SP37424).</strong> A larger, heavier machine with a
              listed 49 by 20-inch belt and a listed weight of 106 lb. It shares the name and little
              else.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The rest of this review covers the 2.25 HP SuperFit 2-in-1 in its single-display forms.
            If you buy through a reseller, match the model number and the speed figures to these
            before you pay, and buy from Costway or the GoplusUS Amazon storefront if you want the
            brand to stand behind it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Goplus 2-in-1 Specifications: Confirmed, Conflicting and Claimed</h2>
<p>
            The table sets each figure against how far it can be trusted. “Confirmed” means the
            brand’s listings and at least one independent hands-on review agree.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Item</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Figure</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Status</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 40 x 16 in. (39.3 x 16 in Costway’s table)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Confirmed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking mode</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.6-2.5 mph, rail folded</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Confirmed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Running mode</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Up to 7.5 mph, rail locked upright</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Confirmed; one review says 8 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Capacity</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">265 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Confirmed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folded thickness</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 5 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Confirmed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Machine weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">69.5, 70, 73 or 74 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Conflicting</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.25 HP, rating type not stated</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listing claim</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Confirmed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">365 days (Costway store) or 3 months (Amazon data)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Conflicting</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A 40-inch belt is a walking belt with room for a jog</h3>
          <p>
            Forty inches by sixteen is typical of 2-in-1 pads and short by treadmill standards. It is
            enough for a walking stride and a short, light jog. Three independent reviewers who used it
            made the same point in their own way: one tester at 5 feet 9 inches had to take
            deliberately short strides, another found it too short and narrow for a 5-foot-9 partner,
            and a third said its taller testers had to shorten their stride to avoid the front or back
            of the belt when running. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            works through stride length against belt length.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The weight question</h3>
          <p>
            The machine’s weight is given as 69.5 lb in one Amazon listing, 73 lb on Costway’s current
            listing and in a newer Amazon listing, 74 lb by one hands-on reviewer and in another Costway
            listing, and about 70 lb by another reviewer. The differences probably reflect versions
            and whether the handles are counted. Either way, call it 70 to 74 lb: light enough to stand
            on end and wheel, heavy enough that several reviewers found moving it harder than they
            expected, and a two-person lift out of the box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rail-and-modes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Fold-Up Rail: How the Two Modes Work</h2>
<p>
            The 2-in-1 design is the reason to buy this machine, and the way it works shapes how you
            will use it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rail down, walking mode.</strong> With the front upright folded flat, the pad is a
            <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pad</a>: about 5 inches thick, able to slide under a standing desk, and limited to
            about 2.5 mph. That cap is the most important number on the machine. A gentle 1.5 to
            2.5 mph is a comfortable typing pace for many people. A brisk walk, for most adults, is
            3 to 4 mph, which this pad cannot reach in desk mode.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rail up, running mode.</strong> Lock the upright and the controller unlocks the
            higher range, up to 7.5 mph. Independent reviewers confirmed that the faster mode only
            becomes available once the rail is locked in place, which is a sensible safety
            interlock: the faster you go, the more you want something to hold. With the rail up, the
            machine is no longer an under-desk pad; it is a small standalone treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The handles.</strong> The two side handles bolt onto the upright. Every hands-on
            reviewer noted that the pad will not fold flat with them attached, so you either unbolt
            them each time you want to store it under furniture or leave them off. One reviewer
            recommended leaving them off if flat storage is the reason you are buying. Our comparison
            of a
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-folding-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad and a folding treadmill</a>
            explains where a 2-in-1 sits between the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="controls-and-display" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Controls, Display, Speaker and App</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The remote is the only control.</strong> There are no buttons on the rail or the
            deck. You switch the machine on at the base, then start, stop and change speed with a
            three-button remote. Three of the four independent reviews we read raised the same
            consequence: lose the remote, or let its battery die, and the treadmill cannot be used;
            the fourth found relying on it clumsy. That reviewer also found the remote uses an infrared signal that needs a line of sight, which is
            awkward with the pad under a desk. Keep a spare battery, and keep the remote in one place.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The safety key.</strong> A magnetic safety key clips under the rail and must be in
            place for the machine to run; pulling it stops the belt. Clip it to your clothing, rather
            than leaving it on the rail, whenever you use the running mode.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The display sits on the deck.</strong> The LED readout is at the front of the
            belt, cycling through time, speed, distance and calories. One reviewer found it hard to
            read from standing height while another found it large and clear, and two noted that many units default to kilometres,
            which can make distance look better than it is. Treat the calorie figure as a rough
            estimate; our page on whether
            <a href="/are-treadmill-calories-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calorie counts are accurate</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The speaker and app.</strong> A built-in Bluetooth speaker plays audio from your
            phone; reviewers described it as fine for the price and no more. Newer versions pair with
            the Gymax app for workout records and speed control. Neither the speaker nor the app
            is needed to use the machine, and there is no subscription.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="owner-reports" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Independent Reviewers and Owners Consistently Report</h2>
<p>
            The SuperFit 2-in-1 has been written about more than most budget pads. Points that came up
            in more than one independent review are these.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>It is compact and easy to store</strong> once the handles are off, and it
              arrives almost fully assembled; reviewers reported setup in about 20 to 25 minutes.</li>
            <li><strong>It is best for walking.</strong> Every reviewer described it as a walking
              machine with a jogging option, not a running treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>The belt is short for taller users.</strong> Reviewers at and above about
              5 feet 9 inches had to shorten their stride.</li>
            <li><strong>Remote-only control is a weakness.</strong> Raised in every hands-on review, most often as the risk of losing it.</li>
            <li><strong>The display sits low on the deck.</strong> Reviewers differed on how much that matters.</li>
            <li><strong>It is heavier to move than it looks.</strong> Two reviewers said the small
              wheels help less than they hoped.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Noise is the one point on which reviewers split. One found it about as quiet as other
            models they tested, with footfall the main sound; another, testing an earlier unit,
            found it louder than their full-size treadmill. That spread is common with walking pads,
            where floor type, <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt lubrication</a> and walking style change the result. Our
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmill guide</a>
            explains where the noise comes from and what reduces it.
          </p>
          <p>
            One reviewer also reported that the motor seemed to struggle when they stood on the belt
            before starting it, and recommended starting the belt first. That is one report, but it
            is good practice on any small pad.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-and-buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Warranty, Returns and Where to Buy</h2>
<p>
            The warranty is the figure that changes most between listings, and on a machine at this
            price it matters as much as the hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Costway’s store</strong> sold the current version for $149.99 on the day we
            checked, advertising free shipping, 30-day returns and a 365-day warranty on the listing.
            <strong>Goplus’s Amazon storefront</strong> (the seller GoplusUS) listed the same deck at
            $189.99 to $199.99, and Amazon’s product data for every Goplus 2-in-1 listing gives the
            warranty as 3 months. An independent review that checked the Goplus website reported the
            same three-month warranty, with returns within 90 days if the machine is in new
            condition. And an older listing for a dual-display version, quoted in an earlier independent
            review, advertised a one-year warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            We cannot tell you which term Costway would apply to an Amazon purchase. On the evidence,
            buying directly from Costway gets you the longest written cover at the lowest price, and
            Amazon gets you Amazon’s own return process. Whichever you choose, save a copy of the
            listing’s warranty wording on the day you buy, and ask the seller in writing if it is not
            clear. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            lists what to ask, and our guide to
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            explains how marketplace sellers affect returns.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the CPSC recall database for Goplus and Costway on 14 October 2026. There is no
            recall of a Goplus or Costway treadmill. Costway has recalled other products: baby
            strollers in 2020, and two Babyjoy high-chair products in 2025, each for violating federal
            safety standards for those products, and Costway-branded portable steam saunas were
            included in a Giantex recall in May 2026. None of those affects the treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="keeping-it-running" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Keeping a Goplus 2-in-1 Running</h2>
<p>
            A budget 2-in-1 spends most of its life doing the hardest job a small motor does: carrying
            a person slowly for long periods. A few habits make a real difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lubricate before first use and on schedule.</strong> One reviewer noted that the
            box includes small bottles of lubricant and that the belt should be lubricated before the
            first session. Follow the manual for the interval after that; our
            <a href="/how-to-lubricate-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad lubrication guide</a>
            covers the method on a low deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Watch the tracking.</strong> At a desk you drift, and the belt follows. Correct it
            early with small turns of the rear roller bolts, as the manual describes, rather than
            letting it rub on the frame. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring a treadmill belt</a>
            walks through it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Break up long sessions and plug it in properly.</strong> Give the motor a rest
            between long desk sessions, and plug the pad straight into a wall outlet rather than a
            thin extension lead, since motors draw a surge at start-up. Our page on
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">extension cords and treadmills</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety basics.</strong> Unplug before cleaning or adjusting, keep children and pets
            away from the belt when it is running or plugged in, and stop using it if it smells of
            burning, surges in speed or trips a breaker.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-it-suits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who the Goplus 2-in-1 Suits, and the Alternatives</h2>
<p>
            <strong>It suits slow desk walking with an occasional jog.</strong> If you want to walk
            gently while working and jog for ten minutes at the end of the day, the two modes do
            exactly that, at a low price, in a machine that disappears under the sofa.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Brisk desk walkers should choose a faster walking mode.</strong> The 2.5 mph cap is
            the main reason to look elsewhere. The pads in our
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads with handrail roundup</a>
            include models whose rail-down mode reaches about 3.8 to 4 mph, and our
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmill guide</a>
            covers flat pads built for long desk sessions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Runners and taller users need a longer belt.</strong> A belt of about 40 inches
            is short for running at any height and for walking above about 5 feet 9 inches. Our
            <a href="/best-low-profile-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">low-profile treadmill roundup</a>
            includes slim full-length machines, and our
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-tall-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads for tall people</a>
            page covers longer pads.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anyone who wants long cover should buy direct or spend more.</strong> If a
            three-month warranty would worry you, buy from Costway’s store, where a 365-day term was
            advertised, or choose a pad from a brand with a longer written warranty. Two other budget
            pads we have read the same way are the
            <a href="/wellfit-tm037-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Wellfit TM037</a>
            and the
            <a href="/treaflow-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Treaflow 3100W</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Goplus 2-in-1 the same as the SuperFit treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in most listings. Goplus sells the 2-in-1 under the SuperFit name, and Costway, the company behind Goplus, sells the same machine on its own store. Several versions exist, including an original single-display model, a newer Gymax-app model, a dual-display model with a 9 mph mode and a larger 4.75 HP machine, so check the model number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run on the Goplus 2-in-1 treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Briefly and lightly. With the rail locked upright it runs up to 7.5 mph, but the belt is only about 40 by 16 inches, and independent reviewers found taller users had to shorten their stride. Treat it as a walking pad with a jogging option rather than a running treadmill, and clip the safety key to your clothing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How fast does the Goplus 2-in-1 go in walking mode?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 2.5 mph with the rail folded down, which is the under-desk mode. That is a comfortable typing pace for many people but below a brisk walk. To go faster you have to raise and lock the rail, which takes the pad out from under a desk. Some reviews quote the same limits in km/h, as 1 to 4.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the 2-in-1 folding treadmill with RC Bluetooth speaker and LED display a Goplus?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually. That title is Costway’s catalogue name for the Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1 family, copied by resellers, often with a Costway code beginning SP37. At least one shop uses a near-identical title for a different 2.0 HP machine rated for 250 lb, so check the model number, and buy from Costway or the Goplus storefront for brand support.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty does the Goplus 2-in-1 have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on where you buy it. Costway’s store advertised a 365-day warranty on the day we checked, while Amazon’s listing data for Goplus 2-in-1 models gives 3 months, and one independent review reported a three-month term on the Goplus website. Save the warranty wording on the day you buy and ask the seller if it is unclear.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use the Goplus 2-in-1 without the remote?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in normal use. There are no speed or start buttons on the machine; the small remote starts it and changes speed, and the safety key only stops it. Every hands-on review flagged this. Keep the remote in a fixed place, keep a spare battery, and ask the seller about a replacement remote before you need one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Goplus 2-in-1</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most disappointment with this machine comes from expecting it to be something it is not. These are the four mistakes to avoid.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a brisk walk under the desk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Walking mode stops at about 2.5 mph. If you planned to walk at 3 to 4 mph while working, you will have to raise the rail, which takes the pad out from under the desk. Check your usual walking speed before buying, and choose a pad with a faster rail-down mode if you need one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a reseller listing because the title matches</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The long “RC Bluetooth speaker and LED display” title is copied by many shops. Some sell the Goplus machine without the brand’s support; at least one sells a different, lower-rated machine. Match the model number, speeds and weight rating, and buy from Costway or the Goplus storefront if you want the brand behind it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every listing carries the same warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Costway’s store advertised 365 days; Amazon’s data gives 3 months. On a budget motor and controller, that difference decides whether a failure at month five is repaired or is your problem. Save the wording from the listing you buy from, and ask in writing if it is not stated.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Losing the remote</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The remote is the only way to start the belt and change speed. Owners who leave it on the desk or the sofa end up with a machine they cannot use until a replacement arrives. Give it a fixed home, keep a spare battery, and find out how to order a replacement while the warranty runs.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

